<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malietoa Tanumafili I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malietoa Tanumafili I was a Samoan paramount chief, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malietoa-laupepa">Malietoa Laupepa</a>. After his father's death in 1898 he was declared king, but his succession was disputed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mataafa-iosefo">Mataʻafa Iosefo</a>, and the ensuing war and foreign intervention led to the abolition of the kingship by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripartite-convention-1899">Tripartite Convention of 1899</a>. He later served as an adviser to the German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> administrations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malietoa-tanumafili-i.jpg" alt="Malietoa Tanumafili I" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malietoa_Tanumafili_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
